{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kw57v","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yvvV6","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4072600954","outdated":true,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=9e123eff-7dd1-4fa4-8569-1cfea8f8b1d6 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=fa9eb8c9-0eaf-591d-a2c2-a7604d30b0af --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: Include file mode and symlink-type metadata in the worktree snapshot comparison; path records and bytes alone allow a chmod-only change to pass as stable. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kw576","path":"apps/vscode/package.json","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yvvWH","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4072600967","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=5366c02f-8ba0-4ecf-9dcd-31655a4c576f --> <!-- cubic:review-run=fa9eb8c9-0eaf-591d-a2c2-a7604d30b0af --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":6} --> P2: `diffpanel` and `@diffpanel/presentation` are ESM-only packages (`\"type\": \"module\"`, exports expose only the `import` condition), but this app is a CommonJS package that typechecks under `module: \"NodeNext\"` (tsconfig.base.json). Value imports like `import { titleForScope } from \"diffpanel\"` (src/tree.ts:2) and `import { REVIEW_TITLE_MAX_LENGTH } from \"diffpanel\"` (src/extension.ts:3) run in a CJS resolution context, so `tsc --noEmit` raises TS1479 (ESM module cannot be imported with `require`), breaking `pnpm validate`. esbuild masks this at runtime by bundling, but the typecheck gate fails. Fix by setting `moduleResolution: \"Bundler\"` (with `module: \"ESNext\"`) in apps/vscode/tsconfig.json since esbuild owns emit, or by adding a CJS/`require` condition to both packages. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kw58A","path":"apps/vscode/src/review-groups.test.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yvvWQ","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4072600976","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=06f929a6-3d78-4af6-9184-50a4b00f1412 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=fa9eb8c9-0eaf-591d-a2c2-a7604d30b0af --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":8} --> P2: This test now runs against the built package artifact instead of the source under test. `@diffpanel/presentation`'s `exports` map points at `dist/index.js` and there is no vitest alias in the repo redirecting package imports to `packages/presentation/src`, so `groupFiles`/`partitionChanges`/`diagramSourceError` etc. (moved out of this app into `packages/presentation/src/grouping.ts`/`diagram.ts`) are exercised via the tsup bundle. `dist/` is gitignored, so on a clean checkout `pnpm --filter diffpanel-vscode test` fails until `packages/presentation` is built, and after editing that source the test silently runs stale output. `packages/presentation/src/presentation.test.ts` already tests the same functions against source (`./index.js`), so this suite's coverage is duplicated on the wrong target. Add a vitest alias mapping `@diffpanel/presentation` to `../../packages/presentation/src/index.js` (and `diffpanel` to `../../packages/core/src/index.ts`) for tests, or move the test into the presentation package. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kw58-","path":"packages/generation/src/index.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yvvXr","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4072601067","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=6f375cfe-da0d-4e46-90f0-17dd7a69a1e1 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=fa9eb8c9-0eaf-591d-a2c2-a7604d30b0af --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":6} --> P3: This package's public API exposes zod objects (generatedReviewSchema, plus validateGeneratedReview whose result derives from zod calls), but zod is not declared as a dependency or peer dependency of @diffpanel/generation — consumers only get it transitively through diffpanel. For a published reusable package this leaves the zod copy unpinned at the API boundary (a later zod major bump in diffpanel would silently change the shapes this package promises, and consumers matching on ZodError get a reversed version coupling). Declare zod explicitly as a peerDependency (and devDependency for local typing/tests) so the version contract is visible to consumers. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kw59m","path":"packages/presentation/src/presentation.test.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yvvYm","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4072601126","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=1f580b6e-b85d-4144-a13d-0d90533d4a81 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=fa9eb8c9-0eaf-591d-a2c2-a7604d30b0af --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":7} --> P3: `match()`'s `source`/`target` parameters are never passed a non-default value (every call is `match(\"a\")`, `match(\"b\")`, `match(\"mixed\")`), so the test never covers what happens when items rewrite to *different* sources. `partitionChanges` groups `first` and `second` only because both happen to map the identical `@/old` → `@/new`; the outcome for distinct rewrites staying in `individual` is unverified. Either drop the parameters or add a case where two items rewrite to different sources and assert they stay individual. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kyFoy","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yxg0Q","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073065744","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Avoid full integrity recovery on every store open**  When the library contains large snapshots, the VS Code provider polls `list` every four seconds (`apps/vscode/src/tree.ts:20-22`), and every resulting CLI process reaches this call before it can answer. `recover()` takes an exclusive `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` lock, reads and hashes every referenced blob in every run, and scans the blob tree for garbage; pagination repeats that work in a separate process for each page. Once this scan takes longer than the polling interval, list processes accumulate while prep and publish wait on the writer lock, making the extension and CLI progressively unusable. Gate the expensive integrity/GC pass behind explicit or infrequent maintenance rather than running it for every read command.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kyFo7","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yxg0a","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073065754","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Keep filesystem deletion outside the rollback scope**  If pruning encounters an error after one of these `rm` calls, the outer maintenance transaction rolls the database deletions back but cannot restore the run directories already removed. A concrete case is repository-scoped pruning when another repository has an unreadable retained manifest: the scoped preflight succeeds, these directories are deleted, then the global `garbageCollectBlobsLocked()` fails on that unrelated manifest, restoring rows whose manifests and reviews no longer exist. Preflight all potentially failing global work before deletion, or commit the row removal before performing idempotent filesystem cleanup.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kzK37","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yzI03","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073491767","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Backfill legacy blob references during migration**  When upgrading an existing schema-v3 library, this creates an empty `run_blobs` table but does not populate it from existing manifests. The CLI and VS Code content path opens the store with `recover: false`, while `getRunBlob()` now requires a matching row, so every pre-upgrade review fails to open file content with “not referenced” until an unrelated recovery-running command happens to backfill the table. Populate these references as part of migration or lazily authorize and backfill them from the requested run's manifest.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kzK4E","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yzI1D","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073491779","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Reject oversized worktree files before reading them**  For a large modified or untracked regular file, this reads the entire file into a `Buffer` before `captureChangedFile()` compares its length with `maxFileBytes`. Consequently the new capture budget does not bound memory or I/O for worktree files; a multi-gigabyte file can exhaust the Node process even with the default 2 MiB limit. Use the already-read file metadata to reject oversized files before calling `readFile()`, while still guarding against size changes during the read.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6kzK4L","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7yzI1L","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073491787","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Serialize the review-title schema migration**  When two commands first open the same schema-v3 library concurrently, both can read `PRAGMA table_info(runs)` before either performs this `ALTER TABLE`; after one adds the column, the other attempts the same statement and fails with a duplicate-column error. This is plausible during an upgrade while the extension polls and another CLI command starts. Run the inspection and alteration under a database migration transaction/lock, or recheck after acquiring the write lock.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6k0Lnp","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7y0pn4","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4073888248","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Verify conflicted staged captures against index changes**  When the index contains any unmerged entry, `resolveScope()` omits `indexSha`, but this branch still returns every staged capture after a single pass because only worktree captures are verified. If an editor or another Git process stages a conflict resolution while the per-file `git show` and `git diff --cached` calls are running, the resulting snapshot can combine paths, content, and patches from different index states…162 tokens truncated…y executes, so `list --repository` can no longer access that repository's persistent review history. The same canonicalization is used by `prune --repository`, preventing cleanup of those runs as well; preserve canonicalization for existing paths while falling back to the resolved path for unavailable repositories.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lJJ9U","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zUoFl","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4082270565","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Retry conflicts resolved by deleting the path**  The new index-stability comparison does not cover a conflict resolved as a deletion: if `git rm` removes an unmerged path after discovery but before this `git show :path`, Git reports `path '…' does not exist (neither on disk nor in the index)`, which this regex does not recognize. The capture therefore throws immediately instead of reaching the `indexBefore`/`indexAfter` comparison and retrying with the now-stable deletion. Fresh evidence beyond the earlier index-consistency case is this unhandled deletion-resolution diagnostic.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lJJ9c","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zUoFv","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4082270575","outdated":false,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Serialize setters with awaited maintenance**  When one store instance is awaiting filesystem work here while holding `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, a same-process call to synchronous `setArchived()` or `setReviewTitle()` on another instance blocks the Node event loop inside better-sqlite3 while waiting for that lock. The lock-owning operation cannot resume and commit, so the setter stalls for the 30-second busy timeout and then fails with `SQLITE_BUSY`; make these writers participate in the maintenance queue or avoid holding the write transaction across awaits.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lKuWk","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zXAfU","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4082894804","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Skip gitlinks before reading snapshot objects**  For a repository containing a submodule, `git ls-tree -r --name-only` includes the gitlink path, but `git show <sha>:<path>` commonly exits 128 with `fatal: bad object` because the referenced submodule commit is not stored in the superproject. That diagnostic is not recognized here, so `prep --repo` aborts the entire snapshot instead of skipping the unsupported entry; changed submodules can similarly abort other capture scopes. Inspect the tree mode/type and skip gitlinks rather than passing them to `git show`.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lKuWq","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zXAfc","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4082894812","outdated":false,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Queue store opens behind in-process maintenance**  When one store instance is awaiting filesystem work inside `createPreparedRun()`, `recover()`, or another maintenance operation, it holds `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`; a concurrent `DiffpanelStore.open()` for the same home reaches this synchronous immediate transaction before joining `maintenanceQueues`, blocks the Node event loop for the busy timeout, and prevents the lock owner from resuming to commit. Fresh evidence beyond the previously identified setter case is that migration during `open()` still bypasses the new queue, so concurrent users of the public storage package can receive `SQLITE_BUSY` after a long stall.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lMUaG","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zZa02","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4083526966","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Accept legal POSIX repository filenames**  On POSIX, backslashes, carriage returns, and newlines are legal filename characters, and the Git commands above already use NUL-delimited output and argument arrays to handle them safely. Rejecting these characters makes any worktree, staged, range, or repository capture containing such a path abort entirely with `Unsafe repository path`; validate actual traversal and platform-specific separators instead of rejecting legal filename characters unconditionally.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lSSDw","path":"packages/presentation/src/presentation.test.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zipNG","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4085945158","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=c3ed785b-fb50-4afc-a987-532213f07fe6 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=24018860-b656-5027-9d7a-bdff8dcb02ef --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":10} --> P1: This new assertion fails against the current implementation. `importRewrite` returns `null` for `export { value } from \"@/old\";` → `export { value } from \"@/new\";`: in `grouping.ts`, `importLine` rejects every named export binding because the check `/^\\{\\w$,\\s*\\}$/u` can never match — the `$` anchor right after `\\w` forces end-of-input before the closing `}`/`,`/spaces, so `!test(binding)` is always true and the line is discarded as invalid. I reproduced this by running the exact `importLine`/`importRewrite` logic: reexport => null. The side-effect and malformed assertions pass; this one does not. Fix the regex in `grouping.ts` (e.g. `/^\\{[\\w$,\\s]*\\}$/u`) so named reexports parse, or change the expectation to `toBeNull()` if reexport grouping is not intended. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lSXi-","path":"packages/storage/src/store.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zixrg","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4085979872","outdated":false,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Verify immutable content before restoring ready status**  When `open()` recovery marks a prepared run failed because a manifest blob is missing or corrupt, the CLI's `publish` command still reaches this method: `getRun()` validates only the manifest and review, after which the unconditional update changes the run back to `ready`. Publishing therefore reports success even though its saved file content remains unreadable, and the next recovery marks it failed again. Recheck the manifest's blobs before publishing, or reject failed runs unless their failure is known to be review-only.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lSsur","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zjSSi","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4086113442","outdated":false,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Skip oversized Git objects before reading their contents**  When a tracked blob exceeds `maxProcessOutputBytes` (16 MiB by default), this `git show` is terminated before the later `maxFileBytes` check can classify the file as skipped. Consequently, a repository snapshot containing a large image—or a range deleting a large tracked file—aborts the entire capture even though the 2 MiB per-file limit is intended to skip oversized files. Inspect the blob size before materializing it, or bound the read at `maxFileBytes + 1` so it reaches the existing skip path.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lSsuw","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zjSSr","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4086113451","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Normalize Git's locale before matching diagnostics**  On systems where Git emits localized diagnostics, expected missing-object cases no longer match these English-only phrases. For example, capturing an unmerged staged path invokes `git show :path`, and its localized “not at stage 0” error is thrown instead of being treated as absent content; the same affects the newly handled conflict-resolution deletion race. Force a stable locale such as `LC_ALL=C` for Git subprocesses or detect absence without parsing human-readable stderr.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6laZfN","path":"package.json","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zvXXn","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4089279975","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=c252d9b8-4516-46f3-844f-b3b3854334eb --> <!-- cubic:review-run=75e8983e-e70d-516d-9d29-325c28fcceaf --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":7} --> P2: `eslint .` at the repo root lints every JS/TS file the config's `ignores` list misses, and flat config does not read `.gitignore`. The config excludes `**/dist/**`, `**/coverage/**`, `.turbo/**`, `.opencode/**` (the latter added because `.opencode/plugins/entire.ts` is tool-generated), but the other gitignored artifact and tool-output directories stay exposed — Playwright's `playwright-report/` and `test-results/` (created by `pnpm test:browser`) contain bundled JS that fails `@eslint/js` recommended rules, and editor dot-dirs (`.claude`, `.codex`, `.cursor`, `.factory`, `.gemini`, `.entire`, `.pi`) can hold tool-generated `.ts`/`.js` on developer machines. This makes `pnpm lint` (and `pnpm validate`, `corepack pnpm lint` in .github/workflows/publish-tag.yml) depend on local machine state. Align the flat-config ignores with .gitignore: add `playwright-report/**`, `test-results/**`, `*.vsix`, and the remaining tool dot-dirs, or scope the script to tracked files (e.g. `eslint $(git ls-files '*.{js,mjs,cjs,ts,mts,cts,jsx,tsx}')`). "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6laZfU","path":"packages/git/package.json","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zvXXz","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4089279987","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=2f7dddcf-d493-4501-acca-b04d863ca2bb --> <!-- cubic:review-run=75e8983e-e70d-516d-9d29-325c28fcceaf --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":6} --> P3: This script now invokes the `eslint` binary, but `eslint` is not declared in this package's devDependencies — it resolves only because the root workspace hoists it. @diffpanel/git is published to npm, so `pnpm --filter @diffpanel/git lint` and `turbo run lint` should work from the package's own dependencies; declare `eslint` (and `globals`/`typescript-eslint` if the config needs them) in devDependencies so the script is self-contained. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6laZfe","path":"packages/core/package.json","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zvXYA","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4089280000","outdated":false,"body":"<!-- cubic:v=c792111b-d4f9-42b5-8529-139ffe4d22f9 --> <!-- cubic:review-run=75e8983e-e70d-516d-9d29-325c28fcceaf --> <!-- metadata:{\"confidence\":6} --> P3: The `lint` script now runs `eslint src`, but `eslint` is not in this package's devDependencies — it is only a devDependency of the workspace root, where the root script is `eslint .`. Under pnpm's strict node_modules layout, a workspace package script only resolves binaries declared by that package, so `pnpm --filter diffpanel run lint` (and `turbo run lint`) fails with `eslint: command not found`. Note that the previous `tsc --noEmit` script worked because `typescript` is a declared devDependency here. Nothing invokes the per-package lint in `pnpm validate` or CI (root validate uses `eslint .`), so this breakage is not caught anywhere. "}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6labpV","path":"packages/git/src/capture.ts","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7zva1L","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4089294155","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Retry transient worktree read failures**  When a worktree file is renamed, removed, or replaced after discovery but before either capture pass opens it, `readWorktreeFile()` throws an `ENOENT` or “changed during capture” error. This catch retries only conflicted staged captures (`liveIndex`), so worktree captures abort immediately instead of using the remaining attempt; the newly added stability retry only works when both full passes complete. Retry transient worktree-state errors after rediscovering the changed paths, while still propagating cancellation and policy/security failures.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
{"id":"PRRT_kwDOTzN9qc6lk3Z8","path":".github/workflows/publish-tag.yml","comments":[{"id":"PRRC_kwDOTzN9qc7z_XQX","url":"https://github.com/21nCo/diffpanel/pull/1#discussion_r4093473815","outdated":true,"body":"**<sub><sub>![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)</sub></sub>  Target the core release by path instead of its duplicate name**  When publishing `diffpanel-v0.0.2`, `PACKAGE_NAME` is `diffpanel`, which is also the root manifest's name. pnpm filters by manifest name; in this checkout, `pnpm --filter diffpanel exec pwd` selects both the repository root and `packages/core`. Consequently this step runs the root `pnpm test` as well as the core test (and the preceding build has the same problem), expanding into all workspace tests after dependencies were installed with `--ignore-scripts`; because `better-sqlite3` is rebuilt only for a storage release, the required core-first release can fail before publishing. Filter using the resolved package path or give the root manifest a distinct name.  Useful? React with 👍 / 👎."}]}
"}

